    "title": "Somalia's Constitution Deadline Forces Ministers to Choose Between Cabinet and Parliament",
    "original_title": "17 Wasiir iyo 2 Ra’iisul Wasaare Ku-xigeen oo wajahaya kala doorashada Xildhibaannimada iyo Wasiirnimada",
    "slug": "somalia-s-constitution-deadline-forces-ministers-to-choose-between-cabinet-and-parliament",
    "summary": "With constitutional provisions taking effect on October 14, 16 ministers who also serve as lawmakers must resign from one position. The move affects two deputy prime ministers and could reshape both branches of government.",
    "content": "Mogadishu, October 8, 2026 — Somalia's Independent Committee for Constitutional Review and Implementation has announced that constitutional provisions separating parliamentary and ministerial roles will take effect on October 14, 2026. The provisions, specifically Article 81, Clause 1(f) and Article 117, Clause 3(a), require individuals to vacate one of the two positions if they hold both.\n\nAt least 16 ministers in the current cabinet are also members of parliament, including two deputy prime ministers. Among those affected is Ali Yusuf Ali (Hoosh), who serves as Minister of Internal Affairs and is a federal lawmaker. The committee's decision means these officials have days to decide whether to remain in the cabinet or return to their parliamentary seats.\n\nThe constitutional articles were designed to reinforce the separation of powers between the executive and legislative branches, a principle embedded in Somalia's provisional constitution. However, full implementation had been delayed for years due to political sensitivities and logistical challenges. The current deadline follows months of debate over how to enforce the dual-mandate ban without disrupting government operations.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the change carries significant implications. Many diaspora members have invested in Somali politics, either by running for parliament or by serving in ministerial roles, and the dual-mandate ban may alter pathways for political engagement. Additionally, a cabinet reshuffle could affect diaspora-focused policies, such as remittance regulations, dual citizenship rights, and consular services. The outcome may also influence upcoming elections, as lawmakers who choose to stay in parliament will retain their legislative influence, while those who remain ministers will need to be replaced in the legislature.\n\nThe committee has not yet specified how vacancies will be filled or whether replacements will be appointed or elected. Political analysts suggest the decision could trigger a broader government reshuffle, potentially affecting key ministries. Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre has not publicly commented on the matter, but his office is expected to manage the transition.\n\nAs the deadline approaches, all eyes are on Mogadishu to see which officials prioritize their legislative roles and which opt to continue in the executive. The decision will undoubtedly shape Somalia's political landscape in the coming months.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/08/17-wasiir-iyo-2-raiisul-wasaare-ku-xigeen-oo-wajahaya-kala-doorashada-xildhibaannimada-iyo-wasiirnimada/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
